Raise yamux stream window default to 8 MiB; BDP-driven per-session window

The per-stream yamux receive window (1 MiB default) capped single-stream
throughput at window/RTT, and the adaptive boost never engaged on
high-bandwidth/low-RTT links because of its 150 ms RTT gate. 10 of 12
public-IP directions failed the 0.8-vs-bare-TCP gate.

- Default native_initial_window_bytes: 1 MiB -> 8 MiB.
- adaptive_throughput_floor: assume 1 Gbit/s (was 500 Mbit/s), clamp the
  mux window to [1 MiB, 64 MiB] (was [1 MiB, 16 MiB]).
- Client runtime now recomputes the mux window from every RTT sample with
  no engagement gate: max(baseline, BDP floor), only when the operator
  left the window at its default. The 150 ms / 80 ms hysteresis still
  gates the tunnel-buffer and TCP-buffer boosts only.

The window stays a limit, not an allocation (yamux recv_window
accounting; streams start at the 256 KiB protocol constant). No
protocol/wire-format change, no obfuscation change.

Tests: cargo fmt --check, check/clippy --workspace --all-targets
--all-features, and the full workspace test suite all pass, including
new clamp-bound, RTT-mapping, ungated-raise, and monotonicity tests.
The 12-direction throughput matrix runs as the experimental gate after
this commit.
